[BELORUSS.FullName]
Liberation of Belarus
[BELORUSS.Description]
Liberation of Belarus.
Vitebsk–Orsha Offensive, June 23-28, 1944.


[BELORUSS.Epilogue]
The offensive of the 1st Baltic and the 3rd Belorussian Fronts was carried out on June 23-28 as part of the Belorussian Strategic Offensive Operation of 1944. It’s goal was to destroy the left wing of the German Army Group Centre on the Vitebsk-Lepel and Orsha lines of advance. Taking advantage of the marshy-wooded terrain as well as numerous rivers with wide swampy floodplains, the German troops created a 20-45 kilometers deep Panther defensive line. 

The main attack of the 1st Baltic Front on Lepel was to destroy the enemy Vitebsk-Lepel force with the help of the 3rd Belorussian Front. Then they were to force a crossing of the Western Dvina river and secure the area around Lepel and Chashniki. The 3rd Belorussian Front was to strike two blows: one at Bogushevsk, Senno-Vitebsk and another at Borisov and Orsha to destroy the enemy Orsha force and gain access to the Berezina river.

In the morning of June 23, after an artillery prep barrage, the Fronts launched the offensive. During the very first day, the enemy defenses to the northwest and south of Vitebsk were penetrated 10-16 kilometers deep. Further developing the offensive, the troops of the 43rd and 39th Armies liberated Vitebsk on June 26, having encircled a couple divisions of the Third Panzer Army. During this offensive, the enemy lost 20000 killed, 10000 captured and a lot of military hardware. This created a wide gap in the German defenses. In the morning of June 26, in battle zone of the 5th Army, the deep penetration forces of the 5th Guards Tank Army started to advance to Tolochin and Borisov, thus adding to the encirclement of the German forces in Orsha. On June 27, Soviet forces liberated Orsha. Continuing the offensive, by June 28, the 1st Baltic Front reached a line 30 kilometers southeast of Polotsk and Lepel, while the 3rd Belorussian Front reached the Berezina river to the north of Borisov. During this operation, both Fronts cooperated with Belorussian partisans.

After the Vitebsk-Orsha Offensive, the left wing of the Army Group Centre was destroyed. Soviet forces advanced 80-150 kilometers, creating favourable conditions for further encirclement and destruction of the German forces near Minsk.

[Ilychevka.FullName]
Ilyichevka
[Ilychevka.Description]
Liberate Ilyichevka, Gorovatka, Shapechino. 
Eliminate enemy forces in the area.

[Rudaki.FullName]
Rudaki
[Rudaki.Description]
Liberate Rudaki, Osipovo. 
Secure the road to Vitebsk.

[Ordej.FullName]
Ordezh
[Ordej.Description]
Liberate Ordezh, Sosnovaya Naspa and repel enemy counter-attacks. 
Clear the area of enemy troops. Regroup and get ready to advance to Kurtenki-Vysochany.

[Visochani.FullName]
Liberation of Vysochany.
[Visochani.Description]
June 1944, the beginning of Operation Bagration, Bogushevsk sector. Your squad is ordered to liberate Vysochany from the German occupants and destroy the enemy foothold. You must eliminate the enemy garrison in Kurtenki that controls the road to Vysochany; secure this road; destroy enemy armoured troops in the Kurtenki-Vysochany area; and suppress artillery on the southern outskirts of Vysochany.

[Osinovka.FullName]
Osinovka
[Osinovka.Description]
Liberate Osinovka, Gryada, Zarechye.
Eliminate enemy forces in the area.

[Simashkovo.FullName]
Simashkovo
[Simashkovo.Description]
June 24, 1944, the offensive continues. Your squad is ordered to liberate Simashkovo; repel enemy counter-attacks; liberate Shnitki; secure the road to the Krinki station; and prevent the enemy from redeploying reinforcements along this road.
